NNAAMMEE
       whereis - locate the binary, source, and manual page files for a
       command

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       wwhheerreeiiss [options] [--BBMMSS _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y... --ff] _n_a_m_e...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       wwhheerreeiiss locates the binary, source and manual files for the specified
       command names. The supplied names are first stripped of leading
       pathname components. Prefixes of ss.. resulting from use of source code
       control are also dealt with. wwhheerreeiiss then attempts to locate the
       desired program in the standard Linux places, and in the places
       specified by $$PPAATTHH and $$MMAANNPPAATTHH.

       The search restrictions (options --bb, --mm and --ss) are cumulative and
       apply to the subsequent _n_a_m_e patterns on the command line. Any new
       search restriction resets the search mask. For example,

          wwhheerreeiiss --bbmm llss ttrr --mm ggcccc

       searches for "ls" and "tr" binaries and man pages, and for "gcc" man
       pages only.

       The options --BB, --MM and --SS reset search paths for the subsequent _n_a_m_e
       patterns. For example,

          wwhheerreeiiss --mm llss --MM //uussrr//sshhaarree//mmaann//mmaann11 --ff ccaall

       searches for "llss" man pages in all default paths, but for "cal" in the
       _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_m_a_n_/_m_a_n_1 directory only.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --bb
           Search for binaries.

       --mm
           Search for manuals.

       --ss
           Search for sources.

       --uu
           Only show the command names that have unusual entries. A command is
           said to be unusual if it does not have just one entry of each
           explicitly requested type. Thus 'wwhheerreeiiss --mm --uu **' asks for those
           files in the current directory which have no documentation file, or
           more than one.

       --BB _l_i_s_t
           Limit the places where wwhheerreeiiss searches for binaries, by a
           whitespace-separated list of directories.

       --MM _l_i_s_t
           Limit the places where wwhheerreeiiss searches for manuals and
           documentation in Info format, by a whitespace-separated list of
           directories.

       --SS _l_i_s_t
           Limit the places where wwhheerreeiiss searches for sources, by a
           whitespace-separated list of directories.

       --ff
           Terminates the directory list and signals the start of filenames.
           It _m_u_s_t be used when any of the --BB, --MM, or --SS options is used.

       --ll
           Output the list of effective lookup paths that wwhheerreeiiss is using.
           When none of --BB, --MM, or --SS is specified, the option will output the
           hard-coded paths that the command was able to find on the system.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

FFIILLEE SSEEAARRCCHH PPAATTHHSS
       By default wwhheerreeiiss tries to find files from hard-coded paths, which are
       defined with glob patterns. The command attempts to use the contents of
       $$PPAATTHH and $$MMAANNPPAATTHH environment variables as default search path. The
       easiest way to know what paths are in use is to add the --ll listing
       option. Effects of the --BB, --MM, and --SS are displayed with --ll.

